content.text:
A flashback, then: you are younger, and the cargo manifest in your hands is stamped shut with a factory seal that no one on the dock will break. The foreman says the debt your father carried goes with the freight, and that the signature at the bottom is already yours. You never signed it. You count the crates anyway, because counting is easier than arguing, and the foreman watches you do it with the patience of a man who has collected from your family before. Behind the stacked containers, the sealed plant hums on, owed to no one and owing everyone.

